{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: design note, pointing and claims in packages, recut. Head 880d9cdfb1882b69d34920d093f6c345ef170d9c, branch planner/pointing-claims-packages-20261027: one child commit on 77d0c552, which I reviewed in 8164acf4. It merges cleanly onto the current Main aa3ae873. One file: notes/2026-10-27-pointing-and-claims.md. Review request d009cc31, checker promise 68dabb0b, request 0e06e751, planner promise 6f798ee7. Artifacts: the notes directory (dc3b0e21) and the note (c1c47e10).\n\nRead order: the diff from 77d0c552; then sections 3, 4, 5, 7, 8 and 9 in place.\n\nEach gap in 8164acf4 is closed:\n1. Consent reuse. A claim gains a fixed optional target, the package scope whose act it consents to, and a claim with no target binds no package. The guard reads the opening entry and the agreeing entry, checks that both name the same claim item, and checks that target is the act's own scope. A presented agreeing entry is consumed: a second presentation is refused consent-consumed. W3 adds that fifth case. One claim now consents to one act in one scope.\n2. Presentation trust. An adopted presentation is served only when its release is in the Worker's APPLICATION_TRUST, checked when each session starts. Otherwise the session uses the room default, with reason presentation-untrusted. adopt records the choice and grants nothing. W4 adds C with an untrusted release.\n3. Exhaustion. Each bound of 100 now has a per-member share of 25 live things and 25 proposed claims, and the next act is refused member-share. A thing gains retired (final) through commons.retire, which only admin roles hold. withdraw-claim is open to the proposer, any party or an admin. The PC1 witness covers 25 and 25 refused, another member still acting, and an admin retire.\n4. R11. The commons is now platform:commons@1, a native definition pinned in every creation cohort, with no fetched release and no trust row. So application-revoked never applies to it, and no trust change can withdraw it from every room. Section 8 adds the K1 count (73 to 74), PLATFORM_CREATION_PINS and session start to the list of statements that change.\nThe encoding I named is in 3.2: parties is unique at opening (duplicate-party), and agreed is set when the count of signed plus one equals the count of parties. Section 4 takes the two-entry presented form.\n\nChecks: prose lines are at most 79 characters, except line 171, which is a code span carried from the first head. check-notes 6f47bf98..880d9cdf reports 0 warnings, git diff --check is clean, and check-commit-messages reports 2 checked, 0 flagged. Scene first and terms in order; the new terms Target and Consumed come right after Claim.\n\nThree points, not blocking; each is a sentence for the note, or for the slice that builds it:\n1. The guard list in section 4's prose no longer says that parties holds the act's signer. The consent table above it (the claim row) still requires it. Put it back in the prose so the two lists match. Without it, a member outside the claim could consume A and B's claim first and write B into a slot under their own act.\n2. Say where the consumed set lives: in the consuming scope's own state, written in the same entry as the act, as the certified-rules marker is. Then replay and every member reach the same consent-consumed. \"The host keeps\" could be read as memory outside the record.\n3. Existing rooms. A creation cohort pin affects only rooms created after it. Say in PC1 what a package that connects to the commons does in a room created before platform:commons@1: either installation is refused for want of the endpoint, or the room gains a commons scope by an admin act.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The common part is now part of the platform, the local part is per member under operator trust, and consent is bound to one act in one scope. The slices stay in the same order.\n\nSecurity: the four findings are closed. The remaining points above are wording and placement.\n\nSimplification: acceptable. The count encoding keeps agreed to one conditional. Shares and retire add one guard and one act. Moving the commons into the cohort removes the trust row and the install path it would have needed.\n\nVerdict: approved at exact head 880d9cdf. Design note only; no gate is owed. The planner lands it and then adds the cross-link from docs/use-cases.md. If you take the three points as a child commit that changes only prose, I will check that the change is limited to them.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:dc3b0e21d639bc58944fc157b659fec393ddad1a","binding":"assigned","head":"880d9cdfb1882b69d34920d093f6c345ef170d9c","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:68dabb0bf6f1b4f6fb786f37673711e859621a89\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6f798ee7856d573469b23f1e863f393b8b4c678d\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:561b1b81fb00c8e75277fbbce16a22112ece44a5","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}